---
import DocsPage from '../../components/DocsPage.astro'
import { sourceUrl } from '../../data/source-projection'
import { sitePath } from '../../data/site-path'
---

<DocsPage
  title="React adapter source"
  eyebrow="PRE-RELEASE FRAMEWORK REFERENCE"
  summary="Render a caller-owned projection in React while the workbook boundary remains explicit."
  alternateLocaleHref={sitePath('zh/docs/react/')}
>
  <div class="docs-guide">
    <section class="docs-guide-callout docs-guide-callout-warning" aria-label="React adapter status">
      <p class="docs-guide-label">STATUS: REPOSITORY SOURCE ONLY</p>
      <p>
        <code>@einfach/react-excel</code> is a private workspace package at version <code>0.0.0</code>. It is not on npm,
        does not ship a default Worker or WASM backend, and is not a drop-in spreadsheet application. Do not add an npm
        install command to your product documentation yet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2>1. Install the repository workspace</h2>
    <p>The React adapter is consumed from this repository today, so install the workspace once before running its fixture.</p>
    <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/allroad88888888/einfach-excel.git
cd einfach-excel
pnpm install`}</code></pre>

    <h2>2. Run the verified React fixture</h2>
    <pre><code>{`NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1 \
pnpm --filter @einfach/react-excel run e2e`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      This command starts the repository's Vite fixture, opens Chromium, drags from one controlled cell to another, and
      verifies the projected selection. A successful run reports one passing React adapter test.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Understand the available boundary</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-terms">
      <article><strong>Controlled grid</strong><span>Render a visible cell projection that your application already owns.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Provider hooks</strong><span>Bridge selection, navigation, editing, clipboard, and history to UI-core atoms.</span></article>
      <article><strong>Explicit backend</strong><span>Supply your own <code>SpreadsheetBackend</code>; no default data or compute runtime is hidden.</span></article>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Compose one controlled projection</h2>
    <p>This is a repository-source pattern, not a package-install recipe. The React host owns the backend, cells, and requested window.</p>
    <pre><code>{`import type {
  CellRange,
  DisplayCell,
  SpreadsheetBackend,
} from '@einfach/spreadsheet-ui-core'
import {
  SpreadsheetGridView,
  SpreadsheetUiProvider,
} from '@einfach/react-excel'

type ProjectionProps = {
  backend: SpreadsheetBackend
  cells: readonly DisplayCell[]
  window: CellRange
}

export function ReactSpreadsheet({ backend, cells, window }: ProjectionProps) {
  return (
    <SpreadsheetUiProvider backend={backend}>
      <SpreadsheetGridView cells={cells} window={window} />
    </SpreadsheetUiProvider>
  )
}`}</code></pre>

    <h2>5. Keep these responsibilities in the host</h2>
    <ol class="docs-guide-steps">
      <li>Keep the backend instance stable for the lifetime of one workbook; replacing it deliberately creates a new UI core.</li>
      <li>Fetch or derive the bounded projection, then pass its <code>cells</code> and <code>window</code> to the controlled view.</li>
      <li>Use the nearest-provider hooks when you add interaction. Do not create a second React state store for spreadsheet product state.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>6. Inspect the interactive reference</h2>
    <div class="docs-guide-next">
      <a href={sitePath('demos/react-controlled-projection/')}><strong>Run the React projection demo</strong><span>Drag across its fixed 4×4 grid and inspect the selected range. →</span></a>
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/excel-site/src/islands/ReactAdapterDemoIsland.tsx')}><strong>Read the exact host source</strong><span>It supplies a deterministic backend, store, projection, and pointer selection. →</span></a>
      <a href={sourceUrl('excel/react-excel/README.md')}><strong>Read the package boundary</strong><span>See the supported primitives and the deliberate non-goals. →</span></a>
    </div>

    <h2>What is not ready</h2>
    <p>
      There is no published React release, default Worker factory, WASM packaging, browser support promise, or turnkey shell.
      For a production integration today, use the supported <a href={sitePath('docs/getting-started/')}>Solid path</a> or
      implement the <a href={sitePath('docs/backend-port/')}>backend port</a> while the React adapter matures.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsPage>
